.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_set
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_set
functions set the structure
.Fa s
to the time represented by the time_t value
.Fa t .
If
.Fa s
is NULL a new time structure is allocated and returned.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_adj ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_adj
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_adj
functions set the time structure
.Fa s
to the time represented by the time
.Fa offset_day
and
.Fa offset_sec
after the time_t value
.Fa t .
The values of
.Fa offset_day
or
.Fa offset_sec
can be negative to set a time before
.Fa t .
The
.Fa offset_sec
value can also exceed the number of seconds in a day.
If
.Fa s
is NULL a new structure is allocated and returned.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set_string ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_set_string
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_set_string
functions set the time structure
.Fa s
to the time represented by string
.Fa str
which must be in appropriate ASN.1 time format (for example
YYMMDDHHMMSSZ or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ). If
.Fa s
is NULL this function performs a format check on
.Fa str
only.
The string
.Fa str
is copied into
.Fa s .
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set_string_X509
sets
.Vt ASN1_TIME
structure
.Fa s
to the time represented by string
.Fa str
which must be in appropriate time format that RFC 5280 requires, which
means it only allows YYMMDDHHMMSSZ and YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ (leap second is
rejected), all other ASN.1 time format are not allowed.
If
.Fa s
is NULL this function performs a format check on
.Fa str
only.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_normalize
function converts an
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME
or
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME
into a time value that can be used in a certificate.
It should be used after the
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set_string
functions and before
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print
functions to get consistent (i.e.
GMT) results.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_check ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_check
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_check
functions check the syntax of the time structure
.Fa s .
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_print
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_print
functions print the time structure
.Fa s
to BIO
.Fa b
in human readable format.
It will be of the format MMM DD HH:MM:SS[.s*] YYYY GMT, for example "Feb
3 00:55:52 2015 GMT", which does not include a newline.
If the time structure has invalid format it prints out "Bad time value"
and returns an error.
The output for generalized time may include a fractional part following
the second.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print_ex
provides
.Fa flags
to specify the output format of the datetime.
This can be either
.Sy ASN1_DTFLGS_RFC822
or
.Sy ASN1_DTFLGS_ISO8601 .
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_to_tm
converts the time
.Fa s
to the standard
.Vt tm
structure.
If
.Fa s
is NULL, then the current time is converted.
The output time is GMT. The
.Em tm_sec ,
.Em tm_min ,
.Em tm_hour ,
.Em tm_mday ,
.Em tm_wday ,
.Em tm_yday ,
.Em tm_mon
and
.Em tm_year
fields of
.Vt tm
structure are set to proper values, whereas all other fields are set to
0. If
.Vt tm
is NULL this function performs a format check on
.Fa s
only.
If
.Fa s
is in Generalized format with fractional seconds, e.g.
YYYYMMDDHHMMSS.SSSZ, the fractional seconds will be lost while
converting
.Fa s
to
.Vt tm
structure.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_diff
sets
.Em *pday
and
.Em *psec
to the time difference between
.Fa from
and
.Fa to .
If
.Fa to
represents a time later than
.Fa from
then one or both (depending on the time difference) of
.Em *pday
and
.Em *psec
will be positive.
If
.Fa to
represents a time earlier than
.Fa from
then one or both of
.Em *pday
and
.Em *psec
will be negative.
If
.Fa to
and
.Fa from
represent the same time then
.Em *pday
and
.Em *psec
will both be zero.
If both
.Em *pday
and
.Em *psec
are nonzero they will always have the same sign.
The value of
.Em *psec
will always be less than the number of seconds in a day.
If
.Fa from
or
.Fa to
is NULL the current time is used.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_cmp_time_t
and
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_cmp_time_t
functions compare the two times represented by the time structure
.Fa s
and the time_t
.Fa t .
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_compare
function compares the two times represented by the time structures
.Fa a
and
.Fa b .
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_to_generalizedtime
function converts an
.Vt ASN1_TIME
to an
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME ,
regardless of year.
If either
.Fa out
or
.Em *out
are NULL, then a new object is allocated and must be freed after use.
.Pp
The
.Fn ASN1_TIME_dup ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_dup
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_dup
functions duplicate the time structure
.Fa t
and return the duplicated result correspondingly.
.Sh NOTES
The
.Vt ASN1_TIME
structure corresponds to the ASN.1 structure
.Sy Time
defined in RFC5280 et al.
The time setting functions obey the rules outlined in RFC5280: if the
date can be represented by UTCTime it is used, else GeneralizedTime is
used.
.Pp
The
.Vt ASN1_TIME ,
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME
and
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME
structures are represented as an
.Sy ASN1_STRING
internally and can be freed up using
.Xr ASN1_STRING_free 3 .
.Pp
The
.Vt ASN1_TIME
structure can represent years from 0000 to 9999 but no attempt is made
to correct ancient calendar changes (for example from Julian to
Gregorian calendars).
.Pp
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME
is limited to a year range of 1950 through 2049.
.Pp
Some applications add offset times directly to a time_t value and pass
the results to
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set
(or equivalent).
This can cause problems as the time_t value can overflow on some systems
resulting in unexpected results.
New applications should use
.Fn ASN1_TIME_adj
instead and pass the offset value in the
.Fa offset_sec
and
.Fa offset_day
parameters instead of directly manipulating a time_t value.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_adj
may change the type from
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME
to
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME ,
or vice versa, based on the resulting year.
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_adj
and
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_adj
will not modify the type of the return structure.
.Pp
It is recommended that functions starting with
.Vt ASN1_TIME
be used instead of those starting with
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME
or
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME .
The functions starting with
.Vt ASN1_UTCTIME
and
.Vt ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME
act only on that specific time format.
The functions starting with
.Vt ASN1_TIME
will operate on either format.
.Pp
Users familiar with RFC822 should note that when specifying the flag
.Sy ASN1_DTFLGS_RFC822
the year will be formatted as documented above, i.e., using 4 digits,
not 2 as specified in RFC822.
.Sh BUGS
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_print
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_print
do not print out the timezone: it either prints out "GMT" or nothing.
But all certificates complying with RFC5280 et al use GMT anyway.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print ,
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print_ex ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_print
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_print
do not distinguish if they fail because of an I/O error or invalid time
format.
.Pp
Use the
.Fn ASN1_TIME_normalize
function to normalize the time value before printing to get GMT results.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_set ,
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_set ,
.Fn ASN1_TIME_adj ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_adj
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_set
return a pointer to a time structure or NULL if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set_string ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_set_string ,
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_set_string
and
.Fn ASN1_TIME_set_string_X509
return 1 if the time value is successfully set and 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_normalize
returns 1 on success, and 0 on error.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_check ,
ASN1_UTCTIME_check and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_check
return 1 if the structure is syntactically correct and 0 otherwise.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_print ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_print
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_print
return 1 if the time is successfully printed out and 0 if an I/O error
occurred an error occurred (I/O error or invalid time format).
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_to_tm
returns 1 if the time is successfully parsed and 0 if an error occurred
(invalid time format).
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_diff
returns 1 for success and 0 for failure.
It can fail if the passed-in time structure has invalid syntax, for
example.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_cmp_time_t
and
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_cmp_time_t
return -1 if
.Fa s
is before
.Fa t ,
0 if
.Fa s
equals
.Fa t ,
or 1 if
.Fa s
is after
.Fa t .
-2 is returned on error.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_compare
returns -1 if
.Fa a
is before
.Fa b ,
0 if
.Fa a
equals
.Fa b ,
or 1 if
.Fa a
is after
.Fa b .
-2 is returned on error.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_to_generalizedtime
returns a pointer to the appropriate time structure on success or NULL
if an error occurred.
.Pp
.Fn ASN1_TIME_dup ,
.Fn ASN1_UTCTIME_dup
and
.Fn ASN1_GENERALIZEDTIME_dup
return a pointer to a time structure or NULL if an error occurred.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set a time structure to one hour after the current time and print it
out:
.Bd -literal
 #include <time.h>
 #include <openssl/asn1.h>

 ASN1_TIME *tm;
 time_t t;
 BIO *b;

 t = time(NULL);
 tm = ASN1_TIME_adj(NULL, t, 0, 60 * 60);
 b = BIO_new_fp(stdout, BIO_NOCLOSE);
 ASN1_TIME_print(b, tm);
 ASN1_STRING_free(tm);
 BIO_free(b);
.Ed
.Pp
Determine if one time is later or sooner than the current time:
.Bd -literal
 int day, sec;

 if (!ASN1_TIME_diff(&day, &sec, NULL, to))
     /* Invalid time format */

 if (day > 0 || sec > 0)
     printf("Later\en");
 else if (day < 0 || sec < 0)
     printf("Sooner\en");
 else
     printf("Same\en");
.Ed
